TI  - Effect of soundscapes and crowd density on economic behavior in commercial streets
PY  - 2026
T2  - Applied Acoustics
VL  - 242
C7  - 111101
DO  - 10.1016/j.apacoust.2025.111101
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105019196942?origin=resultslist
AD  - College of Horticulture and Landscape Architecture, Yangzhou University, Yangzhou, China
AB  - Urban commercial streets, as critical nodes of economic activity, exert substantial influence on consumer behavior and commercial performance. However, empirical research investigating how soundscape characteristics and crowd density interact to shape economic behaviors in complex urban open spaces remains limited. This study addresses this gap through a case analysis of a typical commercial district in Yangzhou, China, constructing two integrated indicator systems. From a behavioral economics perspective, five economic behavior metrics are developed: Consumption intention intensity (CII), Affective consumption willingness (ACW), Queuing time (QT), Recommendation to friends (RTF), and Revisit intention (RE). Results reveal that both objective acoustic indicators (e.g., LAeq, L10, L50, L90) and perceptual dimensions of the soundscape (eventfulness, calmness, chaoticness) are significantly associated with these behavioral outcomes. Sound source typologies further moderate behavioral responses via perceived loudness, event frequency, and preference. In terms of spatial social density, the study introduces both planar crowd densities (CPD4, CPD8, CPD12) and façade crowd density (CFD) to capture horizontal and vertical perceptions of crowding. Among these, CFD demonstrates strong and consistent correlations with all economic behavior indicators. Based on these findings, the “Sound–Human–Economic (SHE)” model is proposed and validated to conceptualize the interaction mechanisms among acoustic environment, social density, and consumer economic behavior. Sudden sound pressure (SSP), CFD, and Educational level emerge as robust predictors. This research contributes to theoretical advancement in urban acoustic-behavior studies and offers practical implications for evidence-based soundscape design, crowd management, and consumer-centered urban commercial planning. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd.
