AB  - Noise pollution is a worldwide threat affecting humans and animals alike. Particularly, animals that depend on vocal communication or audio signals are found to be the worst sufferers of noise arising from rapidly expanding urbanization and industrialization. Amongst them, birds rely heavily on acoustic communication and cues to perform their everyday functions and remain vigilant against predators. Several reports have featured the negative effects of noise on bird communication. In this paper, we highlight the effect of anthropogenic noise on bird behaviour and bird populations across the globe. We have also cited instances where birds managed to cope with the prevailing urban noise, or appeared to be indifferent to the ambient noise levels. Lastly, we suggest areas of investigation that would help in better understanding of noise-induced effects on avian communities. We also indicate some noise mitigation measures that need to be adopted to minimize the impacts of anthropogenic noise pollution. © 2023 Bombay Natural History Society. All rights reserved.
