AD  - Institute of Urban and Sustainable Development, City University of Macau, SAR, Macau, China
AD  - School of tourism and history and culture, Chizhou University, Anhui, China
AB  - Noise pollution is a critical environmental issue in high-density cities, yet its relationship with urban morphology remains insufficiently understood. Conventional studies typically rely on grid‑based spatial units and linear models, which often oversimplify complex urban forms and fail to capture nonlinear effects, threshold behaviors, and interactions among morphological variables. This study introduces vector-based spatial units combined with a multi-scale analytical framework to improve the assessment of urban morphology-noise relationships in high-density environments. Using Macao SAR, China as a case study, this study integrates the XGBoost regression model with interpretable techniques to reveal the nonlinear effects of urban morphology on noise levels. Compared with grid-based spatial units, the proposed vector-based units better preserve building geometry, enhance cross‑scale stability, and provide stronger explanatory power. Four key urban morphological variables, Building Coverage Ratio (BCR), Percentage of Building Landscape (BPLAND), Building Class Area (BCA), and Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) exhibit significant nonlinear relationships with noise characterized by distinct thresholds and effective ranges. Interaction analysis further demonstrates that combinations of high BCR, BPLAND, and BCA dominate noise amplification, constraining the attenuating effects of NDVI in highly compact settings. These findings deepen the theoretical understanding of morphology-noise interactions and offer actionable guidance for optimizing the acoustic environment in high-density urban areas, highlighting the importance of coordinated regulation of building form and urban greenery to promote healthier and more livable high‑density cities. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
