AD  - School of Rail Transportation, Wuyi University, Jiangmen, 529000, China
AB  - Noise barriers in urban rail corridors are widely deployed to mitigate noise exposure in dense built environments, and these barriers can also modify near-track crosswind conditions that affect operational reliability. However, repeated transitions into and out of discontinuous barrier segments may introduce transient aerodynamic load variations under strong crosswinds, posing reliability relevant operational risks. This study examines the unsteady aerodynamic characteristics of a high-speed maglev train traveling at 600 km/h as it passes through a noise barrier under strong crosswind conditions with wind speeds of 10, 15, 20, 25, and 30 m/s. A three-dimensional unsteady compressible computational fluid dynamics (CFD) approach based on the Improved Delayed Detached-Eddy Simulation (IDDES) method and the shear stress transport (SST) k - ω turbulence model was employed to evaluate the aerodynamic loads during the train’s entry into and exit from the noise barrier-viaduct system and to analyze the spatial-temporal evolution of the flow field and power spectral density (PSD). The results show that under strong crosswinds, the high-speed maglev train exhibits unsteady aerodynamic behavior characterized by load fluctuations and complex vortex interactions that evolve as the train enters, traverses, and leaves the barrier region. Specifically, at high crosswind speeds the noise barrier reduces the head-car side force by over 80% and the overturning moment by over 78%, while the reduction in lift grows from 22.1% to 63% as wind speed increases. The study offers actionable insights for urban planners and transport authorities to enhance the safety and sustainability of high-speed maglev networks in wind-prone metropolitan regions. © 2026 The Author(s).
