AB  - Multi-sensory interactions represent a key pathway through which environmental interventions shape emotional states and perceptual experiences, and may play a regulatory role in supporting students’ physical and mental well-being in university campus settings. This study proposes a “thermal-visual-acoustic” multi-sensory interaction analysis framework. Walking experiments were conducted during transitional seasons across five typical outdoor space types on a university campus in Dalian, China, integrating mobile meteorological monitoring with subjective questionnaires. The total survey route covered 5.6 km and included 22 measurement points. The study aims to examine the differential effects of various outdoor spaces on emotional and perceptual responses and to identify the dominant influencing factors. The results indicate that: (1) The acoustic environment (LAeq) is the key driver of negative affect (NA). (2) Human emotional perception can be enhanced through multi-sensory compensation mechanisms. (3) The multi-sensory interaction effect is regulated with significant spatial heterogeneity across different campus areas. Lake adjacent area exhibited the greatest emotional restoration, while main teaching area showed the strongest emotional stress. (4) The interaction among thermal, visual, and acoustic factors significantly contributed to predicting both positive affect (PA) and negative affect (NA). This study systematically elucidates the interactive effects of multi-sensory environmental on emotion and perception, providing both a theoretical basis and practical implications for the multi-sensory optimization of emotion-supportive campus environments. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
