AB  - Contemporary urban design faces the dual challenge of mitigating thermal stress and enhancing public well-being. While urban morphology's impact on the thermal environment is well-studied, a critical knowledge gap remains in how 3D urban landscape features affect multidimensional human experiences, including physiological states and psychological perceptions. This study proposes an innovative analytical framework based on interpretable machine learning. We performed a fine-grained quantification of 3D landscape features using digital twin technology. Subsequently, Random Forest (RF), integrated with SHAP values and 2D Partial Dependence Plots (PDPs), was employed to deeply decode the dominant drivers and non-linear interaction mechanisms affecting multidimensional human responses—spanning thermal, physiological, and psychological domains. Results indicate significant heterogeneity in drivers and spatial scales: Physiological Equivalent Temperature (PET) is primarily governed by spatial morphology at the proximal micro-scale (10–20 m), such as Sky View Factor and Frontal Area Index. Physiological metrics (Heart Rate) and Vitality serve as direct mappings of thermal stress, controlled by PET and microclimatic parameters. In contrast, emotional responses (e.g., Pleasure, Relaxation) exhibit complex regulation mechanisms. Interaction analysis reveals significant synergies: physically, vegetation cooling efficiency is contingent upon morphological ventilation support (FAI > 5000); psychologically, "blue spaces" elevate the thermal tolerance threshold via a "buffering effect"; while cross-modal interactions uncover bidirectional constraints between the soundscape and thermal environment on relaxation. This study transcends traditional linear assumptions, providing key insights for advancing evidence-based, health-promoting urban landscape design. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
