TI  - Noise reduction during the COVID-19 shutdown alleviated compensatory signalling behavior of male Mountain Chickadees (Poecile gambeli)
ST  - La réduction de bruit pendant le confinement lié au COVID-19 a atténué le comportement de signalisation compensatoire des mésanges de Gambel (Poecile gambeli) mâles
PY  - 2026
T2  - Wilson Journal of Ornithology
DO  - 10.1080/15594491.2026.2636860
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032992094?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Ecosystem Science and Management, University of Northern British Columbia, Prince George, BC, Canada
AD  - Department of Biological Sciences, Thompson Rivers University, Kamloops, BC, Canada
AB  - Anthropogenic noise pollution can negatively impact vocal signals and reduce communication among individual birds; however, with the COVID-19 shutdown, ambient noise levels were drastically reduced. While we were investigating how noise influences the relative audibility of vocalizations to female Mountain Chickadees (Poecile gambeli) breeding in nest boxes along an urban/rural interface, the COVID-19 shutdown occurred, creating an opportunity to determine whether cessation of noise altered male behavior and audibility of their vocalizations to females. During the 2019 and 2020 breeding seasons (Apr–Jul), we tracked males during 50 dawn choruses while passively recording from inside nest cavities in Kamloops, British Columbia, Canada. Prior to the COVID-19 shutdown, the relative audibility of songs (but not calls) decreased as males moved farther from the nest. Urban males responded to increasing noise by remaining closer to their nest, likely maximizing audibility to mates, whereas rural males sang farther from the nest, likely maximizing audibility to neighbors. During the COVID-19 shutdown, ambient noise was drastically reduced (mean reduction of 12.40 ± 1.46 dB from 2019 to 2020), and we detected no differences between habitat types in the distance males sang from the nest or in the relative audibility of signals within the nest box. Our results suggest that anthropogenic noise creates complex dynamics in communication networks, with males facing trade-offs between remaining audible to females within their territory and having their signals transmitted to neighboring birds. However, the birds in our study rapidly altered their behavior with the cessation of noise pollution, suggesting high behavioral flexibility and an ability to rapidly re-acclimate to reduced anthropogenic stressors. © 2026 Wilson Ornithological Society.
