UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/0015809930?origin=resultslist
AD  - United Kingdom
AB  - Until recently, it was thought that everyone reacted in much the same way to noise. However, there is evidence from both laboratory and field studies that this first order approximation is inadequate. Studies carried out at Salford University (U.K.) show there are very large differences between people in the way their annoyance grows with noise. A noise sensitive person notices noise at quite a low level (45 dB(A), typical of a quiet suburban street) while the noise insensitive person doesn't become aware of the noise until it is about 16 times louder (85 dB(A), the level of a busy city street). The first group consists of approximately one fifth of the population, the second group one third, and the rest are considered 'average'. It appears that the distribution of annoyance in the population is not normal but bi modal: there is evidence of two distinct groups with completely different behavior patterns when they are exposed to noise. The implications of the existence of a 'noise sensitive' group are discussed in this report. It is shown that because this group represents about one fifth of the population, their numbers are substantial and existing noise regulations provide little or no protection for them. Based upon an earlier study made at Heathrow airport in London, it has been estimated that in the vicinity of the proposed Maplin airport, at least 21,000 people will be seriously annoyed by aircraft noise, compared with the 500 predicted by planners on the Roskill Commission. Some proposals for the relief of 'noise sensitives' are briefly discussed in the concluding remarks of this report.
